# Line-ending policy: every text file is stored with LF and checked out with
# LF on every platform, Windows included. fslint reads the files on disk and
# rejects CRLF, so a checkout that turned LF into CRLF would fail the lint
# for files nobody touched. Git tells text from binary on its own; the
# binary formats in the tree are named as well so that a misguess never
# rewrites their bytes. (These rules once sat in a file named .gitattribute,
# without the s, which git never read.)
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sln text eol=lf
*.fs text eol=lf
*.fsi text eol=lf
*.fsproj text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.ttf binary

# GitHub's language bar counts every programming-language file it finds, so
# the few here that are not F# -- the Mach fixture generator, the packaging
# scripts, the docs template and the VB sample -- show up beside it. Marking
# them vendored keeps them out of the count, so that the bar reports what the
# project is written in. Data and prose formats (JSON, XML, YAML, Markdown,
# SVG) are not counted to begin with; they are listed all the same so that
# the list is complete.
*.md linguist-vendored
*.json linguist-vendored
*.xml linguist-vendored
*.yml linguist-vendored
*.yaml linguist-vendored
*.svg linguist-vendored
*.fsproj linguist-vendored
*.vbproj linguist-vendored
*.props linguist-vendored
*.targets linguist-vendored
*.slnx linguist-vendored
*.sh linguist-vendored
*.py linguist-vendored
*.html linguist-vendored
*.vb linguist-vendored
*.template linguist-vendored
